{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011jum217MMD1buwBQXTXcgD","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now I have enough context. Let me compile the architectural friction report based on my investigation.\n\n## Architectural Friction Audit — Almanac Bash Loop Engine\n\nBased on my walk through the codebase, I've identified **7 major deepening opportunities** where the architecture could strengthen:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Idempotent Source Boilerplate (High Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 11–75), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 5–30), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh` (lines 22–34), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/worker.sh` (lines 16–35), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/loop-launcher.sh` (lines 28–55)\n\n**Friction**: Each module repeats the same 7-line idiom to idempotently source sibling modules:\n```bash\nif ! declare -F almanac_loop_ui_render >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  __harden_core_dir=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n  source \"$__harden_core_dir/ui.sh\"\n  unset __harden_core_dir\nfi\n```\nEvery file doing 5–7 sibling sources carries 35–49 lines of this noise. Harden-core has 8 dependencies, each with this boilerplate.\n\n**Deletion test**: Pass. This is load-bearing pattern (idempotency guards against re-sourcing in tests), but the repetition is pure ceremony.\n\n**Fix direction**: Extract a single `_almanac_source_idempotent <module_name>` helper in `lib/core.sh` that encapsulates the idiom and resolves paths once.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Shallow Role-Config Wrappers (Moderate Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 94–129, `almanac_harden_role` / `almanac_harden_role_field`), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 51–71, `almanac_converge_role` / `almanac_converge_role_field`)\n\n**Friction**: Two identical wrapper pairs:\n- `almanac_harden_role` (5 lines) → calls `almanac_loop_role_config` + passes provider default\n- `almanac_harden_role_field` (9 lines) → pipes result through awk to extract one field\n- `almanac_converge_role` (5 lines) → identical copy of harden's `role`\n- `almanac_converge_role_field` (8 lines) → identical copy of harden's `role_field`\n\nThe loop-prefix versions in `lib/role.sh` (`almanac_loop_role_field`, `almanac_loop_role_config`) are the real implementation; these are pure pass-throughs adding only the consumer name + default provider.\n\n**Deletion test**: Fail (worth keeping). They declare which roles each loop knows about and embed loop-specific defaults (provider=claude for harden, provider=default for converge).\n\n**Fix direction**: Move the shared default-provider lookup + the awk extraction to `lib/role.sh` as two helpers (one to resolve active provider in role context, one to extract a field from role-config output), so consumers call the seam helpers directly and reduce wrapper boilerplate by 70%.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Tight Coupling: Harden's Hardcoded Dependencies on lib/harden-core (Moderate Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (all 2039 lines), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/cmd/harden.sh` (lines 1–20)\n\n**Friction**: `cmd/harden.sh` only sources `lib/harden-core.sh` (no explicit dependencies on `lib/run.sh`, `lib/agent.sh`, etc.). Those are sourced inside harden-core. If a test or caller wants to unit-test just `harden_rubric_path()` or `harden_role_field()`, it must source the full 2039-line module, dragging in all 8 dependencies (agent, role, feedback, worker, etc.), most of which are unused by that one function.\n\nThe same coupling exists for converge-core (1002 lines sourced as a monolith by `cmd/converge.sh`).\n\nThe CONTEXT.md rule is clear: \"Callers source only the modules they use.\" But the cmd layer breaks this rule: cmd/harden should explicitly source the focused modules it needs, making harden-core itself a consumer, not a barrel.\n\n**Deletion test**: Pass. Harden-core would still exist, just as an orchestrator of focused seams, not a god-module.\n\n**Fix direction**: Refactor harden-core as a thin orchestrator (rubric lifecycle, fanout glue) sourcing focused sub-modules on-demand (rubric.sh, ledger.sh, fanout.sh, ratify.sh, fixer.sh, feedback-gate.sh). Each sub-module has a single concern and is testable in isolation.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Repeated pwd -P Symlink-Resolution Pattern (Minor Friction)**\n**Files**: `lib/agent.sh:23`, `lib/harden-core.sh:12–70` (8 repeats), `lib/converge-core.sh:6–30` (4 repeats), `lib/run.sh:23`, `lib/worker.sh:17`, `lib/loop-launcher.sh:29`\n\n**Friction**: The pattern `__<module>_dir=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd -P)\"` appears 17+ times verbatim. CONTEXT.md notes this *cannot* be factored into a function (bootstrap can't source a lib to find the lib), but it should be a documented standard snippet with one source-of-truth comment in `lib/core.sh`.\n\n**Deletion test**: Pass (the comment refactor doesn't remove code, just clarifies intent).\n\n**Fix direction**: Add a standardized comment block in `lib/core.sh` documenting the pattern with an example, then ensure every module uses it identically (most do, but doc unifies the expectation).\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Harden-Core is Really Three Concerns (High Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 1–750: rubric + role config; 750–1500: ledger + ratify; 1500–2039: fanout + fixer + convergence loop)\n\n**Friction**: The 166 functions in harden-core serve three loosely-coupled concerns:\n1. **Rubric bootstrap** (path, write, approve, accept, snapshot/verify, append-criterion) — reads/writes `rubric.md`, UI-minimal\n2. **Findings ledger** (path, init, append, record, open-blocking, status, parse) — append-only TSV ledger of findings\n3. **Convergence loop** (fanout, ratify, fixer, feedback gate, round loop, hitl checkpoint) — orchestrates workers, agent calls, gate verdict\n\nEach concern has its own prompts, file I/O patterns, and agent integration. Splitting them would isolate testability: a test can mock just the ledger, or just the fanout worker fan-out, without dragging in the full convergence loop.\n\n**Deletion test**: Pass. Each sub-module would still be load-bearing.\n\n**Fix direction**: Extract harden into 3–4 focused modules: `lib/harden-rubric.sh`, `lib/harden-ledger.sh`, `lib/harden-fanout.sh`, `lib/harden-loop.sh` (converge + HITL). Harden-core becomes a 50-line orchestrator dispatcher sourcing them.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Untested Helper Glue in Worker Path Construction (Minor Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh` (lines 150–300: worker path helpers: `almanac_loop_worker_dir`, `almanac_loop_worker_status_file`, `almanac_loop_worker_file`)\n\n**Friction**: These three functions assemble paths like `.almanac/runs/<run_id>/workers/<worker_id>/status.tsv`. They are called by harden-core and worker.sh (7+ call sites), but **tests/test-run.sh does not unit-test them directly** — they're validated only incidentally as part of worker lifecycle tests. A path construction bug (e.g., a trailing slash) could silently break all workers across the system.\n\n**Deletion test**: Fail (load-bearing path algebra; if deleted, callers would inline the path construction, spreading that fragility).\n\n**Fix direction**: Add targeted unit tests to `tests/test-run.sh` exercising the three path functions in isolation, ensuring they handle edge cases (empty run_id, special chars in worker_id, missing .almanac dir).\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Provider Adapters List Enumeration (Minor Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/agent.sh` (lines 48–56: `almanac_provider_list` reads files; lines 59–68: `almanac_provider_known` compares against that list in a loop)\n\n**Friction**: `almanac_provider_known` calls `almanac_provider_list` and iterates the output line-by-line in a while loop to check membership. For each provider lookup (happens on every agent run), the loop spins through the discovered set. It's O(n) per lookup. Harmless at n=2 providers, but the pattern scales badly if the adapter system grows (n=10+).\n\n**Deletion test**: Pass. A minor optimization (memoize the list once at source time, or build an associative array).\n\n**Fix direction**: Cache the provider list at source time in an associative array, or use a single `command -v` check on the adapter file directly instead of iterating the list.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Callback/Hook Inversion: Agent Extract (Minor Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/agent.sh` (lines 165–172: `_almanac_agent_extract`), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/providers/claude.sh` (extract hook), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/providers/codex.sh` (no extract hook)\n\n**Friction**: The `agent_stream` and `agent_capture` shapes call `_almanac_agent_extract` after the provider completes, checking if an `*_extract` hook is defined (codex writes its result directly via argv; claude needs extraction). The hook pattern is correct, but it couples the result recovery to the provider discovery seam — callers can't inject a custom extractor without modifying `lib/agent.sh` or adding a provider adapter.\n\n**Deletion test**: Pass (minor — the current two-provider setup works fine).\n\n**Fix direction**: Minor: document the extract hook contract in the provider-adapter ARCHITECTURE section so future adapters know about it. No code change needed unless a 3rd provider arises with different result recovery.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Opportunity | Files | Friction | Verdict | Direction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Idempotent source boilerplate | lib/{harden,converge,run,worker,loop-launcher}.sh | High | Pass | Extract `_almanac_source_idempotent` helper |\n| 2. Shallow role-config wrappers | lib/{harden,converge}-core.sh + lib/role.sh | Moderate | Fail | Move awk extraction + provider lookup to lib/role.sh |\n| 3. Harden/converge-core tight coupling | lib/{harden,converge}-core.sh (2039+1002 lines) | Moderate | Pass | Split into focused sub-modules (rubric, ledger, fanout, loop) |\n| 4. Repeated pwd -P pattern | 17 sites in lib/*.sh | Minor | Pass | Document standardized pattern in lib/core.sh |\n| 5. Harden-core conflates 3 concerns | lib/harden-core.sh (rubric, ledger, loop) | High | Pass | Extract into 3–4 focused modules |\n| 6. Untested worker path construction | lib/run.sh (path helpers) | Minor | Fail | Add unit tests to test-run.sh |\n| 7. Provider membership check O(n) | lib/agent.sh (almanac_provider_known) | Minor | Pass | Memoize or direct file-existence check |\n| 8. Extract hook convention underdocumented | lib/providers/*.sh | Minor | Pass | Document extract contract in ARCHITECTURE.md |\n\n**Deepening order** (high-impact first): Opportunities **1 → 5 → 3 → 2** would reduce the module surface by ~20%, improve testability, and clarify the engine's internal seams."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2788a3dc-ec0d-4660-89bb-3c9bc3797157","uuid":"026cd1e1-102e-42d3-9987-46a9f69b551c","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:56:25.257Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Walk the `lib/` directory and related entry points of the almanac codebase at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac. The codebase is a bash-based loop engine. Read CONTEXT.md to understand the domain vocabulary (Loop, Runner, Provider, Agent run, Run, Slug, Launcher, Hub, Overseer, provider adapter, loop adapter, role/lens, seams).\n\nI need to find **architectural friction and deepening opportunities** — modules that are shallow (interface ≈ implementation complexity), pure-function extractions that don't earn their keep, tight coupling across seams, untested/hard-to-test code.\n\nApply the **deletion test**: if you deleted module X, would complexity concentrate (it was earning its keep) or just move around (it was a pass-through)?\n\nSpecifically:\n1. Read CONTEXT.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md.\n2. List `lib/` and read each file: `lib/core.sh`, `lib/ui.sh`, `lib/role.sh`, `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/loops.sh`, `lib/loop-launcher.sh`, `lib/feedback.sh`, `lib/run.sh`, `lib/worker.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `lib/converge-core.sh`, `lib/providers/*.sh`, `lib/loops/*.sh`, `lib/almanac-core.sh`.\n3. Look at entry points: `bin/almanac`, `cmd/*.sh`, runner scripts in skills (`scripts/once.sh`, etc).\n4. Look at tests: `tests/*.sh`.\n5. Note duplication, awkward seams, shallow modules, things that *feel* hard to follow as a reader.\n\nReport back: 5-10 specific deepening opportunities. For each: \n- which files\n- the friction (shallow/duplication/tight coupling/untestable)\n- a one-line direction of the fix\n- the deletion-test verdict\n\nBe specific with file paths and line numbers. This is an audit, not a fix — don't propose interfaces in detail, just identify the candidates. Under 1500 words.","agentId":"adf57db138a9cfc74","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now I have enough context. Let me compile the architectural friction report based on my investigation.\n\n## Architectural Friction Audit — Almanac Bash Loop Engine\n\nBased on my walk through the codebase, I've identified **7 major deepening opportunities** where the architecture could strengthen:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Idempotent Source Boilerplate (High Friction)**\n**Files**: `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 11–75), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 5–30), `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh` (lines 22–34), `/Users/ja